WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Resource problems need observability before they become outages.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:13.560
A leak, queue buildup, pool bottleneck, or cleanup failure may develop gradually, and the first user-visible symptom may be far away from the original cause.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.380
Production signals make these issues easier to see and respond to early.

4
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:34.900
Useful signals include memory growth, garbage collection pressure, handle count, socket usage, connection pool pressure, queue length, background job failures, timeout trends, cancellation rates, file cleanup failures, lock contention, and retry behavior.

5
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.400
The exact signals depend on the application, but the review should cover every major resource the system consumes.

6
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.920
Logs should capture safe context.

7
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:58.820
Operation name, resource type, failure category, correlation ID, environment, tenant-safe identifier, dependency name, and cleanup outcome can help investigations without exposing sensitive material.

8
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.560
Structured logging makes it easier to search and alert on resource behavior.

9
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:16.000
Do not log secrets, tokens, connection strings, credential-bearing URLs, raw sensitive content, temporary file contents, or internal paths without redaction review.

10
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:22.900
Diagnostics can spread quickly into monitoring systems, support tickets, dashboards, and exported incident data.

11
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.560
Redaction should be designed before a resource problem forces emergency troubleshooting.

12
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:45.760
Production review should identify resource owners, lifetimes, disposal paths, timeout behavior, cancellation paths, concurrency bounds, retry policies, cache limits, cleanup jobs, health checks, dashboards, alerts, and runbooks.

13
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:52.500
The goal is not more noise; it is a small set of useful signals that support early detection and safe response.
